1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,080
A plumbing failure creates a sinkhole that can swallow a mansion.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:09,980
A dance party turns deadly when designer skywalks collapse.

3
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:15,000
A dangerous cloud of gas explodes into a fiery industrial disaster.

4
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,420
Dykes and levees give way to floodwaters that claim the lives of millions.

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:28,100
And the miracle mineral turns out to be an invisible killer that suffocates thousands each year.

6
00:00:28,101 --> 00:00:33,100
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:33,101 --> 00:00:47,101
Liquefied Natural Gas, or LNG, has become an increasingly popular energy choice for many nations.

8
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:59,080
Though LNG has enjoyed a relatively safe track record, it can be an extremely dangerous commodity, capable of causing massive fires.

9
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:03,240
LNG is natural gas in a liquid form.

10
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:07,900
When natural gas is cooled to minus 259 degrees Fahrenheit,

11
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:11,180
it becomes a clear, colorless, odorless liquid.

12
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:16,100
By liquefying the natural gas, you reduce its volume by a factor of about 640,

13
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:18,040
so you make it much, much smaller in volume,

14
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:21,000
and it allows you to store it much more economically.

15
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:27,200
This property of LNG allows for cost-effective transport of massive quantities of natural gas.

16
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:38,080
Today, more than 150 LNG ocean tankers transport over 110 million metric tons of LNG annually to ports around the world.

17
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:47,080
By 2010, LNG is projected to fulfill 10% of U.S. energy needs.

18
00:01:47,081 --> 00:01:58,980
There are currently 70 storage facilities called peak shaving plants in North America.

19
00:01:58,981 --> 00:02:01,820
They store LNG for peak demands.

20
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:08,780
We also have five LNG import terminals and one export terminal in the U.S.

21
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:14,480
By 2010, we probably will have five or six more import terminals.

22
00:02:14,481 --> 00:02:19,520
The concept of liquefying natural gas dates back to the 19th century.

23
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:23,420
Its practical advantages were, and still are, appealing.

24
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:30,580
Sixty years ago, the city of Cleveland built the first commercial LNG facility in the U.S.

25
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:36,480
It was there that the full scope of LNG's deadly potential would be realized.

26
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:43,980
We're standing here on the epicenter of where the Issa Howell Gas Company exposed an affair occurred in 1944.

27
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:55,890
And it's indeed on this very site, on the green grass of what is now Gardena Park, where the four enormous liquefaction, degasification, natural gas storage tanks once

28
00:02:55,891 --> 00:02:56,591
stood.

29
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:03,060
On October 20th, 1944, a storage tank at the East Ohio Gas Company failed.

30
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:05,816
Suddenly, at 2.41 p.m.

31
00:03:05,817 --> 00:03:12,552
on an otherwise unremarkable fall day, the world ended in the Northwood St.

32
00:03:12,553 --> 00:03:13,540
Clair area.

33
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:19,960
Leaking LNG vapor ignited, and the resulting explosion and fire killed 128 people.

34
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,180
79 homes, 2 factories, and 217 cars were destroyed.

35
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:30,980
The East Ohio Gas Company began building LNG facilities in 1940,

36
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:35,900
as intense wartime manufacturing contributed to repeated natural gas shortages.

37
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,460
So what they would do essentially was, when the demand was low,

38
00:03:40,461 --> 00:03:45,740
They would take on gaseous natural gas, liquefy it and store it.

39
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:52,720
And when the demand was high, they would then re-gasify the liquefied natural gas and use it in that time.

40
00:03:53,680 --> 00:04:03,280
Three 57-foot-tall liquid natural gas tanks, each capable of storing 50 million cubic feet of LNG, were put into operation by early 1941.

41
00:04:03,281 --> 00:04:15,580
Those three tanks were of spherical construction, and the support was from vertical columns that connected up to an equatorial beam that went around those spheres.

42
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:29,700
That plant operated just fine with those three tanks for a couple of years, and the demand increased, and the plant then obtained approval to construct a fourth tank.

43
00:04:29,701 --> 00:04:37,260
The number four tank held 100 million cubic feet of LNG, double the amount of the previous tanks.

44
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:42,200
It was a cylindrical shape and more stable because it was supported from the base.

45
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:49,820
As with the first three tanks, number four was constructed using a 3.5% nickel steel alloy.

46
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:54,340
All of the tanks were built on East Ohio gas property,

47
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:59,420
situated adjacent to the densely populated Cleveland neighborhood called Norwood.

48
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:07,560
There was no templative experience for people, I think, either at the technical level or the neighborhood level,

49
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:11,700
to have any understanding of the danger that they were inviting in their neighborhood.

50
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:13,000
At about 2.30 p.m.

51
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:21,520
on October 20, 1944, several residents of Norwood noticed white, steam-like vapors creeping out from tank number four.

52
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:27,420
Leaking gas from the tank ignited, causing a massive explosion and fire.

53
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:31,500
Houses eight blocks away from the tanks instantly began to burn.

54
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,200
When I first saw the fire, and the only time I saw the fire,

55
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:38,880
is when I was turning down, I was running down East 61st Street,

56
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:43,820
turned around and looked back, and I saw this sky full of fire, full of flame.

57
00:05:43,821 --> 00:05:48,620
And it was like the sun had come to Earth.

58
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:53,320
The burning and the fires reached temperatures of 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

59
00:05:53,321 --> 00:05:58,420
the flames will at their heat probably 2,800 feet in the air.

60
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:04,500
If LNG leaks out of its sub-zero container, it will not immediately dissipate.

61
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:11,300
In its liquid form, natural gas is heavier than air, and it will stay close to the ground.

62
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:16,460
The LNG can linger for up to 30 minutes before warming enough to evaporate,

63
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:19,920
ample time for an ignition source to spark an explosion.

64
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:25,920
The tank in Cleveland contained 90 million cubic feet of LNG, which spread as an invisible

65
00:06:25,921 --> 00:06:28,920
vapor cloud into the street and sewer system.

66
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:35,920
Essentially, the neighborhood just before the first initial explosion occurred is best

67
00:06:35,921 --> 00:06:38,920
thought of as sort of a saturated wick.

68
00:06:38,921 --> 00:06:45,920
Pilot lights ignited the cloud once it was inside, and houses burned from the inside out.

69
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:50,820
Firefighters were helpless as the water supply system was decimated by multiple explosions.

70
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:55,040
The fire continued to smolder for three days after the explosion.

71
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:58,520
The spread of the fire was attributed to several causes,

72
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:02,820
but one fatal flaw was to blame for the initial leak in tank number four.

73
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:06,940
The properties of 3.5% nickel-steel alloys,

74
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:10,660
when subjected to sub-freezing temperatures, were not thoroughly understood.

75
00:07:10,661 --> 00:07:17,860
It was later learned that a higher concentration of nickel was needed to ensure steel's integrity in sub-freezing conditions.

76
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:25,420
The steel alloy lining of tank number four was weakened by the cold temperatures of the LNG, which allowed a crack to form.

77
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:33,360
There was a small leak, and the crack propagated, the leak got large, and it dumped the entire tank.

78
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:39,980
Today, LNG tanks are made with either stainless steel or a steel alloy with 9% nickel.

79
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:43,000
both of which maintain their strength at freezing temperatures.

80
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:49,240
Other fundamental improvements have also become standard procedure in modern LNG facilities.

81
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:58,400
One of the major changes since Cleveland is that facilities are very well instrumented for any hazards.

82
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,500
And this is for vapor, any escaped vapor, any fires, the flame detectors,

83
00:08:05,501 --> 00:08:11,500
such that you can detect immediately any hazard.

84
00:08:11,501 --> 00:08:19,480
And this is part of the philosophy of trying to anticipate any troubles as soon as possible before they happen.

85
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:25,400
Since the Cleveland tragedy, LNG tanks are surrounded by berms and dikes,

86
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:28,200
which can contain the entire contents of a tank.

87
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:32,780
Additionally, an exclusion zone is determined for each facility,

88
00:08:32,781 --> 00:08:37,460
keeping populated areas at a safe distance, even in the event of an accident.

89
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:41,080
LNG is touted as a safe energy source.

90
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:48,300
But in early 2004, an explosion at an export facility in Scykta, Algeria, killed 27 people.

91
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:53,220
There's no question that an accident of the magnitude of the Scykta accident

92
00:08:53,221 --> 00:08:58,580
raises questions associated with the safety of LNG.

93
00:08:58,581 --> 00:09:03,560
but it is premature to read into that in my opinion,

94
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:07,880
given that we just don't know what occurred.

95
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:15,860
Opponents to proposed LNG import terminals fear that terrorists targeting an LNG tanker in port

96
00:09:15,861 --> 00:09:21,720
could turn the vast stores of supercooled gas into a mile-wide ignitable vapor cloud.

97
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:28,540
Recently there has been concern about intentional damage to a facility.

98
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:35,320
Any feasible attack would have results that are less than the maximum credible accident

99
00:09:35,321 --> 00:09:40,420
that the facility and exclusion zones are already designed for.

100
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:44,560
Offshore import terminals are being developed as an alternative

101
00:09:44,561 --> 00:09:49,400
to minimize the risk of potential damage, since they would be several miles from land.

102
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:56,000
The Cleveland disaster provided the foundation for the safe handling of liquefied natural gas.

103
00:09:56,001 --> 00:10:07,000
But despite safety measures and technological improvements, the growing number of LNG facilities will continue to create opportunities for another disaster.

104
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:19,360
Since the first cargoes of LNG were shipped on a regular commercial basis in 1964, over 32,000 shipments have been made without a single incident of LNG being lost

105
00:10:19,361 --> 00:10:23,000
through a breach or failure of the ship's tanks.

