164
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,420
Kansas City, July 17, 1981.

165
00:14:09,421 --> 00:14:15,360
close to 1,500 people gathered at the newly built Hyatt Hotel for a dance competition.

166
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:20,600
The hotel was less than a year old and featured innovative architectural designs.

167
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:27,060
At 7.45 p.m., as some danced and others looked down on the festive atmosphere from skywalks,

168
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:30,860
the fourth floor, 60-ton walkway gave way.

169
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:37,540
It landed on the second floor skywalk below, and both walkways fell to the lobby floor.

170
00:14:37,541 --> 00:14:41,080
114 people were killed.

171
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:46,193
This was the single largest structural disaster in terms of loss of life in U.S.

172
00:14:46,194 --> 00:14:46,894
history.

173
00:14:47,860 --> 00:14:53,660
The investigation following the disaster would reveal an accumulation of project management errors,

174
00:14:54,040 --> 00:15:00,520
which ultimately allowed for a simple yet deadly flaw to be installed in the support system of the overhead walkways.

175
00:15:00,521 --> 00:15:09,660
The Kansas City Hyatt was a stunning example of current architectural innovation when it first opened its doors in July of 1980.

176
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:21,940
Well, the Hyatt Hotel in downtown Kansas City was commissioned to be an architecturally interesting and imposing structure.

177
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:27,100
The lobby, when you walked in, was four stories high.

178
00:15:27,101 --> 00:15:33,220
One of the signature features about the Hyatt Hotel in Kansas City was the fact that they had a set of suspended walkways.

179
00:15:33,620 --> 00:15:40,280
They were there to capture the attention of visitors that walked in because they looked like they were virtually suspended in air.

180
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:44,420
But few realized that the hotel had a problematic history.

181
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:51,580
This hotel was envisioned as a large part of the downtown redevelopment and was commissioned on a fast track.

182
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:52,760
Get it up.

183
00:15:52,761 --> 00:16:00,220
The hotel then went through a fast track in which we built this whole hotel between 1978 and 1980.

184
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:09,340
We built a huge hotel which consists of the underground parking garage and cleaning the tower in a period of two and a half to two and three quarter years.

185
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:17,660
The fast track method, which is still used today, entails continuing to design components of a structure as others are already being built.

186
00:16:17,661 --> 00:16:22,660
The expedited construction of the Hyatt was further complicated by staffing problems.

187
00:16:22,661 --> 00:16:33,660
In 1979, construction of the hotel is going apace, but certain management and personnel problems show up.

188
00:16:40,363 --> 00:16:50,467
Somehow, because of the problems of fast track, because of the changes on the design team, the designer, the design engineer, the engineer of record, and the chief

189
00:16:50,468 --> 00:16:54,000
detailer resigned somewhere in the middle of the project.

190
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:56,300
This project was transferred to somebody else.

191
00:16:56,760 --> 00:17:03,360
During these personnel shifts, a minor design change in the connections supporting the atrium walkways was overlooked.

192
00:17:03,660 --> 00:17:09,380
In the original blueprints, a single metal rod ran through a hollow steel beam called a box beam.

193
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:13,320
The rod carried the weight of each walkway to a roof truss.

194
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:17,780
The original box beam construction was supposed to be a continuous rod.

195
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:25,680
It was supposed to be one rod that went through the fourth floor and picked up the second floor independently.

196
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:33,080
The design was difficult to build, so the steel fabricator made a request to replace the single rod with two separate rods.

197
00:17:33,560 --> 00:17:36,620
thereby creating new connection points in the fourth floor beams.

198
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:41,740
This modification left the second floor walkway hanging from the fourth floor,

199
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:46,860
instead of the ceiling, doubling the load on the fourth floor box beam connections.

200
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:50,300
The new walkway design needed to be drawn in detail,

201
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:52,940
and new weight calculations needed to be included.

202
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:57,620
Several levels of project managers approved the new bridge design,

203
00:17:57,621 --> 00:18:00,920
with the simple yet crucial load calculations missing.

204
00:18:00,921 --> 00:18:05,340
It wasn't that this connection was misdesigned.

205
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:09,000
This connection was never designed.

206
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:11,120
That was the problem.

207
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:18,480
It was detailed, but in changes and movements of the detail between the different parties,

208
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:20,780
it never got engineered.

209
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:25,320
And as it became part of various detailed plans,

210
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:29,320
people kept assuming that the design had been checked at some point in the process.

211
00:18:29,321 --> 00:18:36,380
Hyatt engineer Jack Gillam gave the undesigned connections the final seal of approval, and they were installed.

212
00:18:37,620 --> 00:18:41,760
There was an opportunity to catch the flawed design in October of 1979,

213
00:18:42,360 --> 00:18:45,900
when during construction, a section of the atrium roof collapsed.

214
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,640
Construction was halted, and the building was inspected.

215
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:57,160
We checked all the connections to the roof, but we did not check anything any further at that time.

216
00:18:57,161 --> 00:18:59,640
We did not look at the bridges whatsoever.

217
00:19:00,820 --> 00:19:05,200
So literally, the whole lobby, all of the design details were checked.

218
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:10,740
Even the rods suspending the walkways were looked at.

219
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:15,640
And no one thought to go to where the walkways connected together and down to each other and say,

220
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:20,440
what's that connection and how is it designed?

221
00:19:20,441 --> 00:19:28,500
But unfortunately, and literally, the check stopped one connection too short.

222
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:34,600
In November, construction began again, and the hotel opened the following summer.

223
00:19:37,140 --> 00:19:41,700
Over the next year, the steel box beam connections slowly deformed.

224
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:48,540
On the evening of July 17th, a weekly dance competition was being covered by local news crews.

225
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:53,400
Reporter Michael Mahoney was reaching for a videotape when disaster struck.

226
00:19:53,401 --> 00:20:00,300
I was reaching down, sort of looking at the floor, and heard these really sharp metallic pop, pop, like that.

227
00:20:00,520 --> 00:20:04,440
