655
00:50:47,140 --> 00:50:51,640
Asbestos is the disaster of the century that took a century to uncover.

656
00:50:52,120 --> 00:50:57,540
It claims 10,000 American lives each year and thousands more worldwide.

657
00:50:57,541 --> 00:51:07,540
I can think of no substance in the American workplace that has caused more disease and death than the use of asbestos.

658
00:51:07,541 --> 00:51:21,290
When the World Trade Center towers collapsed in 2001, over 1.2 million tons of identified and unidentified hazardous substances, including asbestos, filled the skies

659
00:51:21,291 --> 00:51:22,540
over Manhattan.

660
00:51:22,541 --> 00:51:33,440
There was enough asbestos in the North Tower of the World Trade Center to create an environmental catastrophe when the building collapsed.

661
00:51:33,441 --> 00:51:39,940
Days after the attacks, the Environmental Protection Agency and Occupational Safety and Health Administration

662
00:51:39,941 --> 00:51:45,740
found no excessive levels of asbestos in the air, except at or near Ground Zero,

663
00:51:46,380 --> 00:51:49,860
and deemed that the air contamination was not a public health threat.

664
00:51:51,100 --> 00:51:53,920
However, that testing may only tell part of the story.

665
00:51:53,921 --> 00:52:00,500
Generally, when these exposures occur, the monitoring that takes place occurs three or four days after the event,

666
00:52:00,501 --> 00:52:04,180
so we don't know what immediately was into the environment

667
00:52:04,181 --> 00:52:08,500
and what immediately people were breathing that were around the event.

668
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:11,960
There were many people who participated in the recovery process,

669
00:52:12,100 --> 00:52:16,660
in the cleanup process, that did not wear proper respiratory equipment.

670
00:52:17,160 --> 00:52:22,240
Many of them have reported the presence of what is known as the World Trade Center cough,

671
00:52:22,900 --> 00:52:29,740
which is a type of acute reaction to the dust that contained all types of toxins.

672
00:52:30,500 --> 00:52:32,140
Including asbestos.

673
00:52:33,400 --> 00:52:41,340
Once touted as the miracle mineral, asbestos in Greek means inextinguishable or incombustible.

674
00:52:42,780 --> 00:52:46,420
The mineral's fire-retardant qualities are among its most valuable characteristics.

675
00:52:47,280 --> 00:52:49,520
But asbestos is also dangerous.

676
00:52:49,940 --> 00:52:59,780
It lurks silently in our walls, pipes, roof shingles, tiles, brake pads, and in the air we breathe.

677
00:53:00,500 --> 00:53:06,500
Asbestos was first mined commercially in Quebec, Canada in the late 1800s.

678
00:53:06,501 --> 00:53:11,500
The rock-like mineral separates easily into very small airborne fibers.

679
00:53:11,501 --> 00:53:16,500
And it is this aerodynamic substance that becomes harmful when inhaled.

680
00:53:16,501 --> 00:53:21,500
These fibers can break down and get into the respiratory tract.

681
00:53:21,501 --> 00:53:25,500
If you don't breathe the fibers in, they're not really a danger.

682
00:53:25,501 --> 00:53:31,780
Those that you can breathe in come into the lung, and because they're natural, they're virtually indestructible.

683
00:53:31,781 --> 00:53:41,920
So they continue in the lung to cause a scarring of the lung, and eventually leads to a breathing problem that we now call asbestosis.

684
00:53:42,760 --> 00:53:51,080
In 1924, results from an autopsy performed on a 33-year-old woman who worked in an asbestos textile factory for 20 years

685
00:53:51,081 --> 00:53:54,880
presented the first clear case of death due to asbestosis.

686
00:53:55,500 --> 00:54:02,120
Then in 1930, the British epidemiological study came about of the textile mill workers

687
00:54:02,121 --> 00:54:08,440
that showed a much higher prevalence of death and disease from asbestosis

688
00:54:08,441 --> 00:54:11,540
in people that manufactured textile products.

689
00:54:12,580 --> 00:54:16,800
Despite this and similar reports, the asbestos industry in the U.S.

690
00:54:16,880 --> 00:54:21,840
continued to mine, manufacture, and use asbestos in over 3,000 consumer products.

691
00:54:21,841 --> 00:54:28,840
The more knowledge that was published in the medical literature about asbestos causing first asbestosis,

692
00:54:29,880 --> 00:54:35,980
then lung cancer, then mesothelioma, then other cancers such as gastrointestinal cancers,

693
00:54:36,360 --> 00:54:41,260
the more asbestos was being produced and sold to the American public.

694
00:54:41,960 --> 00:54:44,780
The industry had long known that asbestos was dangerous,

695
00:54:45,180 --> 00:54:49,700
yet it continued to use it without protecting or warning its employees and consumers.

696
00:54:51,840 --> 00:54:57,180
Insurance companies knew that asbestos was a risk very early on.

697
00:54:57,760 --> 00:55:02,920
In fact, in 1918, an insurance company representative by the name of Hoffman wrote

698
00:55:02,921 --> 00:55:09,100
that insurance companies were reluctant to insure people who mined asbestos.

699
00:55:09,101 --> 00:55:12,840
They didn't know how to describe asbestosis.

700
00:55:13,040 --> 00:55:14,820
They didn't use the term asbestosis.

701
00:55:15,240 --> 00:55:19,880
But they knew that workers in this trade died early, and they died rapidly,

702
00:55:19,881 --> 00:55:23,840
and they died much more often than workers in other industries.

703
00:55:24,500 --> 00:55:27,220
Well, the asbestos industry was aware of this.

704
00:55:27,460 --> 00:55:31,720
During the 1930s, it's very clear that the asbestos industry

705
00:55:31,721 --> 00:55:38,340
were actually sponsoring research into the area of asbestos and asbestos-related disease.

706
00:55:38,680 --> 00:55:41,760
And there are notes in the literature and the letters in the literature

707
00:55:41,761 --> 00:55:47,660
saying this is what we're finding, and when cancer came about as a suspicion,

708
00:55:47,661 --> 00:55:49,540
let's not mention cancer.

709
00:55:49,880 --> 00:55:55,920
Unfortunately, it took decades and thousands of deaths before the industry's dirty little secret was revealed.

710
00:55:57,320 --> 00:56:04,600
But fortunately for future victims and their families, the asbestos industry left a paper trail long enough to hang itself.

711
00:56:05,100 --> 00:56:11,060
So there is just a vast amount of evidence out there that employers knew about asbestos hazards.

712
00:56:11,700 --> 00:56:18,260
Officials of the asbestos companies knew in the 1940s and the 1950s that asbestos could cause cancer.

713
00:56:18,261 --> 00:56:24,300
and in the 1960s they were very well aware that asbestos exposure could cause mesothelioma.

714
00:56:25,180 --> 00:56:29,720
But it wasn't until the late 1960s or the early 1970s

715
00:56:29,721 --> 00:56:33,420
that these companies ever placed warning labels on their products.

716
00:56:34,680 --> 00:56:40,300
Finally, in 1972, when the Occupational Safety and Health Administration was established,

717
00:56:40,301 --> 00:56:43,460
the first substance it regulated was asbestos.

718
00:56:43,461 --> 00:56:52,100
It was virtually impossible for any employer to use asbestos in the workplace to an extent where there was visible dust,

719
00:56:52,720 --> 00:56:58,580
which meant that, as a result, pipe coverings that contained asbestos, asbestos cements,

720
00:56:59,040 --> 00:57:07,320
and all of the various dusty products that asbestos was used in ceased to be commercially viable anymore.

721
00:57:07,321 --> 00:57:12,700
By the early 1980s, companies like Brickley Environmental in San Bernardino, California,

722
00:57:12,701 --> 00:57:21,040
have worked on the remediation of asbestos in schools, office buildings, banks, airports, and other commercial use facilities.

723
00:57:21,320 --> 00:57:27,020
The material that we're actually going to be removing today is the acoustical ceiling texture, which is above us.

724
00:57:27,560 --> 00:57:29,160
This material contains asbestos.

725
00:57:30,020 --> 00:57:33,660
The process for removal will be to get the containment set up,

726
00:57:33,661 --> 00:57:37,080
negative pressure installed, which you can see down at the end of the hallway down there.

727
00:57:37,081 --> 00:57:43,640
The negative pressure system sucks air out of the work area through a series of filters

728
00:57:43,641 --> 00:57:48,920
and discharges that air outside, creating a negative pressure in the work area, which

729
00:57:48,921 --> 00:57:53,400
ensures that asbestos fibers cannot escape the containment.

730
00:57:53,401 --> 00:57:56,980
Once that negative pressure is established, the containment is set up, the decon is set

731
00:57:56,981 --> 00:58:02,920
up, the crew will suit up, put on respirators, come in, wet this material down and manually

732
00:58:02,921 --> 00:58:03,920
begin scraping it.

733
00:58:03,921 --> 00:58:09,280
As they scrape the material, they'll package it, bag it, and process it for final disposal.

734
00:58:10,600 --> 00:58:15,540
Despite the efforts to reduce the amount of asbestos manufactured and used in the United States,

735
00:58:16,120 --> 00:58:18,120
the asbestos crisis is far from over.

736
00:58:18,460 --> 00:58:22,280
Asbestos products continue to be sold today.

737
00:58:22,281 --> 00:58:28,700
Many people think that asbestos has been banned, but in fact, there is no ban of asbestos products.

738
00:58:28,701 --> 00:58:34,820
As long as asbestos remains in the air we breathe, asbestos cases will remain in the courts.

739
00:58:35,760 --> 00:58:42,000
And because of the numbers of people who have been injured by the inhalation of asbestos,

740
00:58:42,460 --> 00:58:45,740
we have hundreds of thousands of cases pending in the court system.

741
00:58:46,480 --> 00:58:52,360
Thousands of men and women who built our country in the decades following World War II have already paid the price.

742
00:58:52,361 --> 00:58:58,200
The people that are dying today are German people that have been exposed 20, 30 years ago.

743
00:59:06,446 --> 00:59:16,546
I think that asbestos has become a disaster in the engineering sense because while it has so many useful uses, the overwhelming problems have been that those are

744
00:59:16,547 --> 00:59:18,240
outweighed by the epidemic.

745
00:59:18,241 --> 00:59:23,280
odorless and tasteless.

746
00:59:24,720 --> 00:59:29,720
This invisible killer has taken over 300,000 lives in the last century

747
00:59:29,721 --> 00:59:36,220
and is estimated to take 150,000 more between now and 2030.

748
00:59:40,120 --> 00:59:42,060
Facts provoke thought.

749
00:59:43,300 --> 00:59:45,220
Thought spills questions.

750
00:59:45,221 --> 00:59:48,920
Questions ignite controversy.

751
00:59:49,820 --> 00:59:51,020
Make up your own mind.

752
00:59:51,580 --> 00:59:52,340
Conspiracy.

753
